== Primary ==
Most attention in the non-partisan primary was focused on Bass, the incumbent Democratic mayor; Pratt, a Republican [[reality television]] personality; and Raman, a Democratic city councilmember.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/08/us/elections/nithya-raman-la-mayor-karen-bass.html|title=Democrat Secures Second L.A. Mayor Spot and Ends Spencer Pratt’s Run|first=Shawn|last=Hubler|date=June 8, 2026|via=NYTimes.com}}</ref> Pratt had come to renewed prominence by channeling popular resentment against city government for its handling of the [[Palisades Fire]], in which he had lost his home the previous year; he became a focal point of Republican support.<ref name="auto"/> Raman, a [[Democratic Socialists of America]] member, entered the race only hours before the filing deadline in order to provide an alternate challenger to Pratt; she positioned herself as more progressive than Bass, focusing on the city's [[Housing crisis in the United States|housing crisis]] and other policy areas where she charged that Bass's establishment politics had failed.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Zhang |first=Christine |last2=Lemonides |first2=Alex |last3=Soler |first3=Caroline |last4=Bloch |first4=Matthew |last5=Hubler |first5=Shawn |date=2026-06-05 |title=See the Areas Where the L.A. Mayoral Candidates Built Their Coalitions |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/06/05/us/elections/los-angeles-mayor-primary-results-precinct-map.html |access-date=2026-06-10 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

