=== Results ===
At the end of election night, Bass led with a plurality of votes, followed by Pratt with Raman in third, with only a fraction of ballots tabulated.<ref name=FayazCNN /> Over the next week, the additional tabulation of ballots narrowed the gap between Raman and Pratt, and the former eventually overtook the latter in vote totals.<ref name=FayazCNN /> With 93% of the vote tabulated on 8 June, election observers concluded that Bass and Raman advanced to a runoff election, with Pratt and all other candidates eliminated.<ref name=APResults>{{cite news|title=Los Angeles, CA Mayor election results|url=https://apnews.com/projects/elections-2026/los-angeles-california-general-results-mayor/|work=Associated Press|access-date=2026-06-08}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/nithya-raman-advances-spencer-pratt-face-l-mayor-karen-bass-runoff-rcna347483|title=Nithya Raman advances over Spencer Pratt to face L.A. Mayor Karen Bass in a runoff|work=NBC News|access-date=2026-06-08}}</ref><ref name=FayazCNN>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/06/08/politics/nithya-raman-spencer-pratt-karen-bass-los-angeles-mayor-election|title=Nithya Raman will advance over Spencer Pratt to face Los Angeles Mayor Karen Bass in November, CNN projects|work=CNN|access-date=2026-06-08}}</ref>

California's practice of incrementally counting ballots and broadcasting updates in daily batches triggered election conspiracists, including [[President of the United States|President]] [[Donald Trump]].<ref name=RectorTimes>{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2026-06-05/how-simple-mix-up-fueled-false-conspiracies-about-la-vote-count|title=How a misreading of data fueled false claims about L.A. mayoral vote count|date=June 6, 2026|website=Los Angeles Times}}</ref> In one batch update, a 41-second delay in the electronic broadcast of Pratt's vote totals relative to Bass's and Raman's led to claims of election fraud.<ref name=RectorTimes /> The LA County registrar's office confirmed to the ''Los Angeles Times'' that Pratt had received additional votes in every batch of updates during the count,<ref name=RectorTimes /> a fact with which the Trump-appointed US attorney [[Bill Essayli]] concurred.<ref name="IyerCNN">{{Cite web |last=Iyer |first=Kaanita |date=2026-06-06 |title=DOJ debunks social media claim of discrepancy in LA mayor vote count while Trump sows doubt in California’s elections {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/06/06/politics/los-angeles-mayor-race-social-media-claims |access-date=2026-06-10 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> The Sunday after the election, Pratt suggested that 43,000 [[Homelessness in Los Angeles|homeless people]] had inappropriately cast ballots for Raman.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gorman |first=Steve |date=2026-06-08 |title=Trump steps up attacks on California's election system |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trump-steps-up-attacks-californias-election-system-2026-06-09/ |access-date=2026-06-10 |work=[[Reuters]]}}</ref><ref name=HublerNYT /><ref name="AnguianoGuardian">{{cite news|last1=Anguiano|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jun/09/spencer-pratt-la-mayor-election|title=How Spencer Pratt’s 'patently absurd' bid for Los Angeles mayor fell flat|first=Dani|date=2026-06-09|access-date=2026-06-10|work=[[The Guardian]]|last2=Ede-Osifo|first2=Uwa}}</ref> On the same day, Trump shut down an interview with [[Kristen Welker]] when she asked him to substantiate his claims of election fraud.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tchekmediyan |first=Alene |date=2026-06-07 |title=Trump, after baselessly alleging fraud in California vote again, storms out of NBC interview |url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2026-06-07/president-trump-storms-off-meet-the-press |access-date=2026-06-10 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref> Federal House Speaker [[Mike Johnson]] also made insinuations of electoral fraud that he was unwilling to substantiate, saying that fraud was "impossible to prove" but could be detected "instinctively".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://newrepublic.com/post/211544/mike-johnson-california-election-fraud-no-proof|title=Johnson Says California Election Fraud Is So Bad It Can’t Be Proven|via=The New Republic}}</ref>

{{As of|2026|6|9|df=US}}, Pratt had yet to concede the race or fulfill his vow to leave Los Angeles if not elected.<ref name=AnguianoGuardian /> On June 11, Pratt asked online, "Are they done counting yet?"; the Associated Press estimated that by that point, 99% of the ballots had been counted and Pratt had received 25.5% of the votes compared to Raman's 29%.<ref name=BourisBeast>{{cite news|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/maga-reality-candidate-spencer-pratt-speaks-out-after-shocker-loss/|title=MAGA Reality Candidate Speaks Out After Shock Loss|work=Daily Beast|last=Bouris|first=Catherine|date=2026-06-12}}</ref> On his syndicated television program, Los Angeles comedian [[Jimmy Kimmel]] announced that he had rented a [[moving truck]] on behalf of Pratt.<ref name=BourisBeast /> On June 12, Pratt released a video announcing that he was no longer encumbered by a campaign, and that "Angelenos are now stuck with two morons responsible for all their problems and now have to choose between dumb and dumber", apparently referring to Bass and Raman; independent observers interpreted the video as a concession.<ref name=KABC>{{Cite web |date=2026-06-12 |title=Spencer Pratt responds to L.A. mayoral race loss, says 'it's war' in new video |url=https://abc7.com/post/spencer-pratt-responds-los-angeles-mayoral-race-loss-video/19283238/ |access-date=2026-06-13 |website=ABC7 Los Angeles |language=en}}</ref><ref name=KarlamanglaNYT>{{Cite news |last=Karlamangla |first=Soumya |date=2026-06-12 |title=Despite Trump Claims, Spencer Pratt Seems to Accept L.A. Mayor Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/12/us/elections/spencer-pratt-la-mayor.html |access-date=2026-06-13 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Pratt also indicated he would not fulfill his vow to leave Los Angeles.<ref name=KABC />

