== Runoff ==
The elimination of Pratt and other candidates set up a runoff election in which Raman challenged the incumbent Bass from the left.<ref name="HublerNYT">{{Cite news |last=Hubler |first=Shawn |date=2026-06-08 |title=Nithya Raman Secures Second LA Mayor Spot, Ending Spencer Pratt's Run |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/08/us/elections/nithya-raman-la-mayor-karen-bass.html |access-date=2026-06-10 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=HublerNYT2>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/30/us/politics/los-angeles-mayor-bass-raman.html|title=Los Angeles Mayoral Candidates Vie for Undecided Voters|last=Hubler|work=New York Times}}</ref> While both candidates are [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]], Raman is also a member of the [[Democratic Socialists of America]], and its Los Angeles chapter endorsed her on September{{nbsp}}2.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-06-08 |title=Nithya Raman advances over Spencer Pratt to face L.A. Mayor Karen Bass in a runoff |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/nithya-raman-advances-spencer-pratt-face-l-mayor-karen-bass-runoff-rcna347483 |access-date=2026-06-10 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> Interviewed at the end of September, Bass continued to be haunted by the Palisades Fire and the [[2026 Boyle Heights warehouse fire]], while Raman was criticized for a previous appearance with left-wing commentator [[Hasan Piker]].<ref name="BloodAP">{{cite news|last=Blood|first=Michael|url=https://apnews.com/article/los-angeles-mayor-karen-bass-nithya-raman-8c9a5b5538d90719924c59b324f2fd0c|title=Raman stresses independence as rivals for LA mayor duel over homelessness, Israel|work=Associated Press|date=September 29, 2026}}</ref> At a forum in [[South Los Angeles|South LA]], the two agreed that the [[Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority]] had failed in its mission and should be jettisoned.<ref name=StoltzeLAist>{{cite news|work=LAist|url=https://laist.com/brief/news/politics/los-angeles-mayor-race-city-council-district-nine-forum-bass-raman-2026-november-election|title= Bass, Raman discuss cops, housing and homelessness|last=Stoltze}}</ref> Raman criticized Bass for being insufficiently aggressive in supporting [[Housing density|housing densification]]; Bass did not support [[SB 79]], which mandates multifamily housing construction near [[rail transit]].<ref name=StoltzeLAist />

Mandated financing disclosures at the end of September, which included some contributions from before the primary election, revealed that Raman's campaign had received $3{{nbsp}}million from a [[hedge fund]] manager who wants more [[bicycle lanes]], and $3{{nbsp}}million from the Western States Regional Council of Carpenters, who felt spurned by the wage terms in one of Bass's previous housing directives.<ref name="GerdaLAist">{{cite news|url=https://laist.com/news/politics/la-mayor-race-bass-raman-donors-campaign-finance-sosnick-airbnb|last=Gerda|title=Donors pour millions more into LA mayor’s race, narrowing gap between Bass and Raman|first=Nick|work=Laist|date=September 25, 2026|last2=Moore|first2=Maloy}}</ref> Bass's campaign had received part of a $4{{nbsp}}million contribution from [[Airbnb]], which was currently in litigation with the city over alleged price gouging after the Palisades and Eaton Fires.<ref name=GerdaLAist /> The [[Los Angeles Police Department]] union spent $3{{nbsp}}million to oppose Raman and several other candidates.<ref name=GerdaLAist />

